# .env.secrets.example — copy to .env.secrets, fill in real values, chmod 600.
# .env.secrets is gitignored; never commit real tokens.
# Save with LF line endings (Unix). CRLF is stripped automatically on copy-in,
# but LF avoids any risk of \r corrupting tokens in editors that don't strip it.
#
# Harness login is handled separately from these service credentials.
# After `floatie create`, use `floatie attach` and follow the installed harness's
# interactive login flow inside the VM. Authenticate each harness separately.

# ── GitHub fine-grained PAT (required for private-repo clone + push) ──────────
# Mint a fine-grained PAT at: https://github.com/settings/personal-access-tokens/new
#
# Required scopes:
#   • Repository access: select the SPECIFIC repos this workspace uses
#     (not "All repositories" — keep blast radius small)
#   • Permissions → Contents: Read and write
#     (Read-only → 403 "write access not granted" on push;
#      repo not selected at all → 404 on clone)
#   • Permissions → Metadata: Read-only  (auto-selected)
#
# Note: GITHUB_TOKEN does NOT need SSH-signing or key-management permissions.
# The signing key is registered as a Signing Key on GitHub (see provision output).
#
# Choose a limited token lifetime and renew it before expiry.
GITHUB_TOKEN=

# ── MCP service keys ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Add one variable per MCP server that needs a secret (token-auth MCPs).
# Reference these in your committed .mcp.json as ${VAR} — e.g.:
#   "headers": { "Authorization": "Bearer ${LINEAR_API_KEY}" }
#
# stdio MCPs that need an API key use the "env" block in .mcp.json the same way:
#   "env": { "MY_SERVICE_API_KEY": "${MY_SERVICE_API_KEY}" }
#
# OAuth MCPs (e.g. the Linear HTTP example in .mcp.json.example) need no key here.
# Authenticate each OAuth server through the installed harness's MCP login flow
# inside the VM. Each harness manages its own MCP credentials.
#
# Examples (uncomment and fill in real values):
# LINEAR_API_KEY=
# MY_SERVICE_API_KEY=
